! HOUSE RULING — what this house takes from the survey, and what it declines

The survey below reads through one ecosystem's vocabulary, because that ecosystem holds the
grammars a TextMate author meets first. Read it for the PATTERN a theme author follows, never for
a name to copy.

''Operator ruling, 2026-09-01:'' this grammar names no language but its own. The survey recommends
the asciidoctor lever — naming markdown's scope beside yours on a construct whose glyph markdown
shares — and measured over 65 themes it carries a list marker from 72% to 91%. The house declines
it. The nineteen points sit entirely in theme rules that spell `punctuation.definition.list.begin.markdown`
by name, and reaching them means telling every reader's editor that a TiddlyWiki bullet belongs to
markdown. A grammar that borrows a language's name to borrow its colour has stopped describing
what it parses.

What the house takes instead: the FAMILY a theme rules on. A list marker reads
`punctuation.definition.list.begin.tiddlywiki5`, the shape markdown, asciidoc and mediawiki all
write, and stands at 72% on its own name.

Measured and declined beside it: `keyword.control.list.tiddlywiki5` reaches 100%, and names a
bullet a keyword. Reach bought by misnaming buys nothing a reader can trust.

A second survey runs wider — across tree-sitter, semantic tokens, Pygments, KSyntaxHighlighting,
font-lock and the rest — so the patterns here stand against systems that share none of this
vocabulary.

