# TextMate scope-naming survey — primary sources only

All files below were downloaded with `curl` and grepped mechanically (`grep -oE 'markup\.[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+'`, `grep -oE 'meta\.embedded[A-Za-z0-9_.-]*'`, plus a Python AST walk over `name`/`contentName` for space-separated values). Working copies in `/tmp/gram/` (not in your repo).

---

## 1. AsciiDoc — asciidoctor/asciidoctor-vscode

The file is **not** `Asciidoctor.json`. Two files, one generated from the other:

- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/asciidoctor/asciidoctor-vscode/main/syntaxes/asciidoc.tmLanguage.base.json (57K, hand-authored)
- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/asciidoctor/asciidoctor-vscode/main/syntaxes/asciidoc.tmLanguage.json (277K, generated by `syntaxes/generate.js`, adds the ~60 fenced-code language blocks)

`scopeName` = `text.asciidoc` (both).

**`markup.*` — 129 distinct in the generated file.** Prose constructs are **bare-language-suffixed `markup.X.asciidoc`**, not Markdown-shaped. Selected: `markup.bold.asciidoc`, `markup.italic.asciidoc`, `markup.heading.asciidoc`, `markup.heading.heading-0.asciidoc` … `markup.heading.heading-5.asciidoc`, `markup.emphasis.constrained.asciidoc`, `markup.emphasis.unconstrained.asciidoc`, `markup.strong.constrained.asciidoc`, `markup.strong.unconstrained.asciidoc`, `markup.monospace.constrained.asciidoc`, `markup.inline.raw.monospace.asciidoc`, `markup.link.asciidoc`, `markup.list.bullet.asciidoc`, `markup.list.todo.asciidoc`, `markup.table.asciidoc`, `markup.block.listing.asciidoc`, `markup.admonition.asciidoc`, plus 70× `markup.code.<lang>.asciidoc`.

**Two Markdown-scoped exceptions** (present in both files):
- `markup.list.numbered.markdown`
- `markup.list.unnumbered.markdown`

**`meta.embedded.*` — only in the generated file**, 61 of them, all `meta.embedded.block.<lang>` (`meta.embedded.block.javascript`, `.typescript`, `.python`, `.shellscript`, `.yaml`, …). The base file has **zero**.

**Multi-scope `name`/`contentName` — 5 distinct, verbatim:**
```
name        = "markup.list.numbered.asciidoc markup.list.numbered.markdown"
name        = "markup.list.unnumbered.asciidoc markup.list.unnumbered.markdown"
name        = "punctuation.definition.list.begin.asciidoc punctuation.definition.list.begin.markdown"
name        = "punctuation.definition.metadata.asciidoc punctuation.definition.metadata.markdown"
contentName = "meta.embedded.block.cpp source.cpp"        (generated file only)
```
The first four live under `repository.list`. The fifth is inherited verbatim from VS Code's own Markdown grammar (see §6).

**This is the exact pattern you're aligning to, and the project stated its rationale in the PR.** https://github.com/asciidoctor/asciidoctor-vscode/pull/1018 — *"feat(grammar): update list and monospace scopes, add snapshot tests"*, body verbatim:

> "Use double scopes on list patterns (e.g. markup.list.unnumbered.asciidoc + markup.list.unnumbered.markdown) so default themes apply colors via the .markdown prefix while users can target the precise .asciidoc scope for customization."

That is the clearest published statement of the "reuse Markdown's names so it looks native, keep your own name for precision targeting" rule I could find anywhere in these projects.

---

## 2. MDX — mdx-js/mdx-analyzer

Path is `packages/vscode-mdx/syntaxes/`. Two files:

- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mdx-js/mdx-analyzer/main/packages/vscode-mdx/syntaxes/source.mdx.tmLanguage (351K, plist XML, `scopeName` = `source.mdx`)
- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mdx-js/mdx-analyzer/main/packages/vscode-mdx/syntaxes/mdx.markdown.tmLanguage.json (1.1K, injection, `scopeName` = `source.markdown.mdx.codeblock`)

### source.mdx.tmLanguage
**`markup.*` — 68 distinct, ALL `.mdx`-suffixed.** No bare Markdown scopes at all:
`markup.heading.atx.1.mdx` … `.6.mdx`, `markup.heading.setext.1.mdx`, `markup.heading.setext.2.mdx`, `markup.quote.mdx`, `markup.inline.raw.code.mdx`, `markup.inline.raw.math.mdx`, `markup.raw.code.mdx`, `markup.raw.code.fenced.mdx`, `markup.raw.math.mdx`, `markup.raw.math.flow.mdx`, `markup.list.table-delimiter.mdx`, plus 51× `markup.code.<lang>.mdx`.

Note there is **no** `markup.bold.mdx` / `markup.italic.mdx` — emphasis is scoped as `string.other.strong.emphasis.asterisk.mdx` etc.

**`meta.embedded.*` — 51, all flat `meta.embedded.<lang>`** (no `.block.` segment): `meta.embedded.js`, `meta.embedded.ts`, `meta.embedded.tsx`, `meta.embedded.python`, `meta.embedded.mdx`, `meta.embedded.yaml`, …

**Multi-scope `name` — 3, verbatim:**
```
name = "markup.raw.code.mdx markup.inline.raw.code.mdx"
name = "markup.raw.math.mdx markup.inline.raw.math.mdx"
name = "carriage-return constant.language.character-escape.line-ending.mdx"
```

The generated grammar's upstream source is **wooorm/markdown-tm-language**, and its `grammar.yml` carries the rationale for those double scopes as an inline comment. https://raw.githubusercontent.com/wooorm/markdown-tm-language/main/grammar.yml, lines 449–450 and 1419–1420, verbatim:

```yaml
      2:
        # GitHub selects `markup.raw`, VS Code uses `markup.inline.raw`.
        name: markup.raw.code.var(suffix) markup.inline.raw.code.var(suffix)
```

That is a *cross-consumer* reuse rule (satisfy Linguist AND VS Code), not a cross-language one — but it is the same mechanism. `var(suffix)` is resolved in `build.js` line 477: `variables.suffix = language.scopeName === 'source.mdx' ? 'mdx' : 'md'` — i.e. the language suffix is systematic and parameterized.

### mdx.markdown.tmLanguage.json
An injection into `L:text.html.markdown`. Uses exactly one `markup.*`, **bare Markdown**: `"name": "markup.fenced_code.block.markdown"`, and `"contentName": "meta.embedded.block.mdx"`. Captures use `punctuation.definition.markdown`, `fenced_code.block.language.markdown`, `fenced_code.block.language.attributes.markdown` — all bare Markdown, because the surrounding document *is* Markdown.

---

## 3. Quarto — quarto-dev/quarto

Path is `apps/vscode/syntaxes/` (not `syntaxes/` at root). Three files; the grammar is authored in YAML and built to plist:

- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarto-dev/quarto/main/apps/vscode/syntaxes/quarto.tmLanguage.yaml (21K, source)
- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarto-dev/quarto/main/apps/vscode/syntaxes/quarto.tmLanguage (233K, built plist)
- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarto-dev/quarto/main/apps/vscode/syntaxes/build-lang.js (generator for the fenced-code blocks)

`scopeName` = `text.html.quarto`.

**`markup.*` in the YAML — 19, all `.quarto`-suffixed:**
`markup.bold.quarto`, `markup.italic.quarto`, `markup.strikethrough.quarto`, `markup.heading.quarto`, `markup.heading.div.quarto`, `markup.heading.setext.1.quarto`, `markup.heading.setext.2.quarto`, `markup.quote.quarto`, `markup.div.quarto`, `markup.line_block.quarto`, `markup.list.numbered.quarto`, `markup.list.unnumbered.quarto`, `markup.raw.block.quarto`, `markup.inline.raw.string.quarto`, `markup.fenced_code.block.quarto`, `markup.math.block.quarto`, `markup.math.inline.quarto`, `markup.underline.link.quarto`, `markup.underline.link.image.quarto`.

**The built plist adds exactly one bare Markdown scope: `markup.fenced_code.block.markdown`** — injected by `build-lang.js`, which emits `name: markup.fenced_code.block.markdown` for every generated `fenced_code_block_<lang>` rule, with capture names `punctuation.definition.markdown`, `fenced_code.block.language.markdown`, `fenced_code.block.language.attributes.markdown`. So Quarto suffixes its own prose but keeps Markdown's names for the fenced-code chrome — the same split MDX's injection makes.

**`meta.embedded.*` — YAML has 2** (`meta.embedded.block.frontmatter`, `meta.embedded.math.quarto`); **built plist has 66**, all `meta.embedded.block.<lang>` (incl. `.mermaid`, `.plantuml`, `.stan`, `.stata`, `.typst`, `.prql`, `.dot`, `.matlab`). Generated at `build-lang.js:487`: `let contentName = \`meta.embedded.block.${language}\`;`

**Multi-scope `name`/`contentName`:** YAML source has **none**. Built plist has **one**:
```
contentName = "meta.embedded.block.cpp source.cpp"
```
Again inherited from VS Code's Markdown grammar. `build-lang.js` explicitly supports appending extra scopes:
```js
let contentName = `meta.embedded.block.${language}`;
if (additionalContentName) {
  contentName += ` ${additionalContentName.join(" ")}`;
}
```
— i.e. Quarto's generator treats space-separated multi-scope `contentName` as a normal, first-class facility.

Quarto also uses hybrid *dot*-composed names in a few captures: `punctuation.definition.quote.begin.markdown.quarto`, `punctuation.definition.list.begin.markdown.quarto`, `meta.image.inline.markdown.quarto` — Markdown's path with `.quarto` appended, which does **not** match a `…markdown` theme selector suffix but does match `punctuation.definition.quote.begin` prefixes.

---

## 4. Twig / Jinja / Liquid

**Twig** — mblode/vscode-twig-language (the "Twig Language" extension). Path is `src/syntaxes/`, not `syntaxes/`:
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mblode/vscode-twig-language/master/src/syntaxes/twig.tmLanguage (78K plist, `scopeName` = `text.html.twig`)
- `markup.*`: **NONE**
- `meta.embedded.*`: **NONE**
- multi-scope `name`/`contentName`: **NONE**

**Jinja** — samuelcolvin/jinjahtml-vscode ("Better Jinja"). 27 grammars in `syntaxes/`, one per host language.
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/samuelcolvin/jinjahtml-vscode/main/syntaxes/jinja.tmLanguage.json (8.4K, `scopeName` = `source.jinja`)
- `markup.*`: **NONE** · `meta.embedded.*`: **NONE** · multi-scope: **NONE**

The relevant one for your problem is the Markdown host:
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/samuelcolvin/jinjahtml-vscode/main/syntaxes/jinja-md.tmLanguage.json — 559 bytes, entire body:
```json
{
  "name": "jinja-md",
  "scopeName": "text.html.markdown.jinja",
  "patterns": [
    { "include": "source.jinja" },
    { "include": "text.html.markdown" }
  ]
}
```
**Zero redefinition.** It gets Markdown's scopes natively by *including* `text.html.markdown` wholesale, and derives its own `scopeName` from Markdown's (`text.html.markdown.jinja`) exactly per the TextMate manual's specialization rule. Sibling `jinja-html.tmLanguage.json` does the same with `text.html.basic` → `text.html.jinja`.

**Liquid — two well-used grammars, both `markup.*`-free:**

https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Shopify/liquid-tm-grammar/main/grammars/liquid.tmLanguage.json (28K, `scopeName` = `text.html.liquid`)
- `markup.*`: **NONE**
- `meta.embedded.*`: `meta.embedded.block.css`, `meta.embedded.block.html`, `meta.embedded.block.js`, `meta.embedded.block.json`, `meta.embedded.block.liquid`
- multi-scope: **NONE**

https://raw.githubusercontent.com/panoply/vscode-liquid/master/syntax/liquid.tmLanguage.json (24K, `scopeName` = `source.liquid`)
- `markup.*`: **NONE**
- `meta.embedded.*`: no *assigned* scope; the only occurrence is an **injection selector key**, verbatim: `"R:meta - (meta.embedded.*.html, meta.embedded.*.*.liquid)"`
- multi-scope: **NONE**

And its Markdown story is the injection route, same as Jinja's — https://raw.githubusercontent.com/panoply/vscode-liquid/master/syntax/injections/markdown.tmLanguage.json, entire body:
```json
{
  "scopeName": "source.markdown.liquid",
  "injectionSelector": "L:text.html.markdown",
  "patterns": [
    { "include": "source.liquid#comment-line" },
    { "include": "source.liquid#comment-block" },
    { "include": "source.liquid#tags" },
    { "include": "source.liquid#output" }
  ]
}
```

**Finding across all three template languages: none of them names any prose construct.** They are pure delimiter/expression grammars that compose with a host prose grammar by `include` or `injectionSelector`. They contribute nothing to the "how do I name prose" question — but they are a strong data point that composition-by-include beats redefinition when the host grammar already exists.

---

## 5. Vue SFC — vuejs/language-tools (confirmation of your prior fetch)

https://raw.githubusercontent.com/vuejs/language-tools/master/extensions/vscode/syntaxes/vue.tmLanguage.json (33K, `scopeName` = `text.html.vue`)

- **`markup.*`: ZERO.** Confirmed.
- **`meta.embedded.*`: ZERO.** Confirmed. Vue does **not** use the `meta.embedded.*` convention at all.
- **Multi-scope `name`/`contentName`: ZERO.**

What it uses instead for embedded regions: **the target grammar's own scope name is assigned directly as `name` (or `contentName`), and the same grammar is `include`d inside.** Exact strings, from the full `name`/`contentName` enumeration:

| region | mechanism | exact scope string |
|---|---|---|
| `<template>` (default) | `name` on the tag rule | `meta.template-tag.start` / `meta.template-tag.end`, body via `#html-stuff` |
| `<template lang="html">` | `contentName` | `text.html.derivative` |
| `<template lang="md">` | `name` + `include` | `text.html.markdown` |
| `<template lang="pug">` | `name` + `include` | `text.pug` |
| `<script lang="ts">` | `name` + `include` | `source.ts` |
| `<script lang="tsx">` | `name` + `include` | `source.tsx` |
| `<script>` / jsx / coffee | `name` + `include` | `source.js`, `source.js.jsx`, `source.coffee` |
| `<style>` variants | `name` + `include` | `source.css`, `source.css.scss`, `source.css.less`, `source.sass`, `source.stylus`, `source.postcss` |
| other blocks | `name` + `include` | `source.json`, `source.json5`, `source.json.comments`, `source.yaml`, `source.toml`, `source.graphql` |
| interpolation/attr expressions | `name` | `source.ts.embedded.html.vue`, `source.css.embedded.html.vue`, `expression.embedded.vue` |

The canonical shape, verbatim from `repository["multi-line-script-tag-stuff"]`:
```json
{ "begin": "(?<=>)", "while": "^(?!\\s*<\\/script[\\s>])",
  "name": "source.ts", "patterns": [ { "include": "source.ts" } ] }
```

Note `source.ts.embedded.html.vue` / `source.css.embedded.html.vue` — Vue puts `.embedded.` *inside* a `source.*` path rather than using `meta.embedded.*`. That is a deviation from VS Code's documented recommendation (§6c).

Two companion injections in the same directory do use bare Markdown scopes, because they inject *into* Markdown:
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/vuejs/language-tools/master/extensions/vscode/syntaxes/markdown-vue.json — `injectionSelector: "L:text.html.markdown"`, `"name": "markup.fenced_code.block.markdown"`, captures `punctuation.definition.markdown` / `fenced_code.block.language.markdown` / `fenced_code.block.language.attributes.markdown`, body `{"include": "text.html.vue"}`. (Sibling `mdx-vue.json` likewise.)

---

## 6. Published guidance — what I could and could not verify

### (b) Multiple space-separated scopes in one `name` — SANCTIONED BY IMPLEMENTATION, NOT BY PROSE DOCS

I found **no published prose statement** from Microsoft, Shiki, or TextMate that documents or blesses multi-scope `name`. I checked the VS Code Syntax Highlight Guide, the TextMate 1.x manual's Language Grammars page, and Aerijo's widely-cited grammar guide — **all three are silent on it**; the TextMate manual and Aerijo's guide show only single dot-separated names. State that plainly rather than citing something that doesn't exist.

What **is** verifiable is the engine implementation. https://github.com/microsoft/vscode-textmate/blob/main/src/grammar/grammar.ts, `AttributedScopeStack.pushAttributed`, lines 535–550 verbatim:
```ts
public pushAttributed(scopePath: ScopePath | null, grammar: Grammar): AttributedScopeStack {
    if (scopePath === null) {
        return this;
    }

    if (scopePath.indexOf(' ') === -1) {
        // This is the common case and much faster
        return AttributedScopeStack._pushAttributed(this, scopePath, grammar);
    }

    const scopes = scopePath.split(/ /g);
    let result: AttributedScopeStack = this;
    for (const scope of scopes) {
        result = AttributedScopeStack._pushAttributed(result, scope, grammar);
    }
    return result;
}
```
Space-separated scopes in `name`/`contentName` are split and pushed as **separate stack entries**, deliberately, with a fast path for the single-scope case. Shiki uses vscode-textmate, so this holds there too.

**First-party precedent:** VS Code's own bundled Markdown grammar ships exactly one such value — https://raw.githubusercontent.com/microsoft/vscode/main/extensions/markdown-basics/syntaxes/markdown.tmLanguage.json contains `"contentName": "meta.embedded.block.cpp source.cpp"`. That single string is the ancestor of the identical value found in both AsciiDoc and Quarto above.

**Portability caveat, verified secondhand only:** search results report that Atom does not support space-separated multi-scopes on a single capture. I did not verify that against Atom source (repo is archived), so treat it as unconfirmed. For VS Code/Shiki targets it is irrelevant.

### (a) Reusing Markdown's scope names so a prose language looks native

No project publishes a document titled anything like "golden rules". The verifiable statements, strongest first:

1. **asciidoctor/asciidoctor-vscode PR #1018** (https://github.com/asciidoctor/asciidoctor-vscode/pull/1018) — quoted in full in §1. This is the only statement I found that names the exact technique *and* its two-part motive (default themes color via the `.markdown` scope; users target the precise `.asciidoc` scope).

2. **VS Code Syntax Highlight Guide** (https://code.visualstudio.com/api/language-extensions/syntax-highlight-guide), verbatim:
   > "In order to have your grammar as broadly supported as possible, try to build on existing scopes rather than defining new ones."

3. **wooorm/markdown-tm-language `grammar.yml`** lines 449 and 1419, verbatim: `# GitHub selects \`markup.raw\`, VS Code uses \`markup.inline.raw\`.` — the reuse motive stated for a *consumer* split rather than a *language* split. `grammar.yml`'s header (lines 1–104) documents variables, rule injection, conditions, oniguruma-vs-PCRE, and inspirations — **it does not state a scope-naming convention**; the WebFetch of the repo README likewise found none.

4. **TextMate 1.x manual, Language Grammars** (https://macromates.com/manual/en/language_grammars) — on `name`: *"the name which gets assigned to the portion matched. This is used for styling and scope-specific settings and actions, which means it should generally be derived from one of the standard names."* On specialization: derive your `scopeName` from the type you specialize (`text.html.markdown`, `text.html.rails`). This sanctions the **`scopeName` derivation** (which `jinja-md` and Liquid follow exactly) but says nothing about duplicating another language's `markup.*` names on individual rules.

### (c) `meta.embedded.*`

**VS Code Syntax Highlight Guide**, verbatim:
> "It is a good idea to always wrap embedded language in a `meta.embedded.*` scope to make sure VS Code treats the embedded language properly."
> "To override this behavior, you can use a `meta.embedded.*` scope to reset VS Code's marking of tokens as string or comment content."

Vue is the one surveyed grammar that ignores this.

---

## Cross-cutting pattern (the actual answer to "bare or suffixed?")

Every surveyed prose grammar suffixes its **own** constructs (`markup.bold.quarto`, `markup.heading.atx.1.mdx`, `markup.italic.asciidoc`) and uses **bare Markdown scopes only at two seams**:

1. **Fenced-code chrome** — `markup.fenced_code.block.markdown` + `punctuation.definition.markdown` + `fenced_code.block.language.markdown`, used bare by Quarto's generator, MDX's injection, and Vue's markdown injection alike.
2. **Constructs whose glyph is identical to Markdown's** — AsciiDoc's `-`/`*` and numbered lists, which is precisely where it reached for the space-separated dual scope rather than picking one.

`meta.embedded.*` shape is not uniform: `meta.embedded.block.<lang>` (AsciiDoc, Quarto, Shopify Liquid, VS Code Markdown) vs flat `meta.embedded.<lang>` (MDX) vs absent (Vue, Twig, Jinja, panoply Liquid).

