# Scope-naming prior art — report

Handoff meme written verbatim (spirit's text untouched, Markdown-flavoured as handed back) at `/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/VSCode-TW5-Syntax/docs/scope-naming-prior-art.mem` (21,095 bytes, `#scope-naming-prior-art`, uncommitted).

## (A) Authoritative naming vocabulary

Two primary sources, and **they disagree on one string**.

**TextMate 1.x manual** (https://macromates.com/manual/en/language_grammars) — 11 root groups: `comment` `constant` `entity` `invalid` `keyword` `markup` `meta` `storage` `string` `support` `variable`. Verbatim on `name`: *"the name which gets assigned to the portion matched … it should generally be derived from one of the standard names."*

**Sublime scope naming reference** (https://www.sublimetext.com/docs/scope_naming.html) — 14 roots (adds `punctuation`, `source`, `text`). Its `markup.*` list verbatim:

```
markup.heading   markup.list.unnumbered   markup.list.numbered
markup.bold      markup.italic            markup.underline
markup.inserted  markup.deleted           markup.underline.link
markup.quote     markup.raw.inline        markup.raw.block
markup.other
```

**The divergence, measured:** Sublime documents `markup.raw.inline`. VS Code's Markdown grammar emits `markup.inline.raw.string.markdown` and every VS Code theme rules on `markup.inline.raw`. Neither `markup.changed` nor `markup.strikethrough` nor `markup.fenced_code.block` appears in either doc, yet all three are widely styled. **The documented vocabulary is a subset of the effective one** — the docs are not sufficient on their own. wooorm's grammar states the split in a comment: `# GitHub selects markup.raw, VS Code uses markup.inline.raw.`

VS Code's own guidance (https://code.visualstudio.com/api/language-extensions/syntax-highlight-guide) adds no list, only the rule: *"In order to have your grammar as broadly supported as possible, try to build on existing scopes rather than defining new ones."* Shiki runs the same engine, so it inherits this.

## (B) What themes actually style — measured, not folklore

I grepped 9 themes (VS Code `dark_vs`/`light_vs` — the boilerplate most themes fork — plus bundled Monokai, Solarized Dark, Quiet Light, and third-party Dracula, One Dark Pro, Night Owl, GitHub Dark).

| scope | themes styling it |
|---|---|
| `markup.heading` `markup.bold` `markup.italic` | **9/9** |
| `markup.inline.raw` | **9/9** |
| `markup.inserted` `markup.deleted` `markup.changed` | **9/9** |
| `markup.quote` | 7/9 |
| `markup.strikethrough` | 6/9 |
| `markup.underline` | 6/9 |
| `markup.underline.link` (+`.image`) | 4/9 |
| `markup.list` | 3/9 |
| `markup.fenced_code.block` | 1/9 (Dracula) |
| `markup.link` | 1/9 |
| **`markup.other`** | **0/9** |

`dark_vs` additionally styles two Markdown punctuation scopes by exact name — `punctuation.definition.quote.begin.markdown` and `punctuation.definition.list.begin.markdown` — and three TextMate-1 legacy aliases still carried by Dracula and One Dark Pro: **`emphasis`, `strong`, `header`** (bare, no dots).

**The finding that matters: `markup.other.*` is as dead as `meta.*`.** It is sanctioned by Sublime's doc and reached by zero themes. Being in the documented vocabulary does not mean being styled.

## (C) `meta.*` — the claim is CONFIRMED, by both primary sources

- **Sublime**, verbatim: *"These are not intended to be styled by a color scheme, but used by preferences and plugins."*
- **TextMate manual**, verbatim: *"A minimal theme will only assign styles to 10 of the 11 root groups below (`meta` does not get a visual style)."*

Not folklore. Two independent authoritative statements. Themes do carry a handful of `meta.*` rules (`meta.embedded`, `meta.preprocessor`, `meta.diff.header`, `meta.object-literal.key`), but these are named exceptions, never a general contract — nothing generic like `meta.heading.*` will ever be reached.

One counter-pull worth keeping: VS Code *requires* `meta.embedded.*` for embedded regions — *"It is a good idea to always wrap embedded language in a `meta.embedded.*` scope to make sure VS Code treats the embedded language properly."* That is behavioural, not colour.

## (D) Mixed prose/code prior art

The cross-cutting pattern across AsciiDoc, MDX, Quarto, Org-mode, LaTeX, Vue, Jinja, Liquid, Twig:

**Every prose grammar suffixes its own constructs and reaches for bare Markdown names at exactly two seams.**

1. **Fenced-code chrome** — `markup.fenced_code.block.markdown`, `punctuation.definition.markdown`, `fenced_code.block.language.markdown` used bare by Quarto's generator, MDX's injection, and Vue's markdown injection alike.
2. **Constructs whose glyph is identical to Markdown's** — AsciiDoc's lists, where it reached for the dual scope rather than choosing.

Concrete shapes: AsciiDoc `markup.bold.asciidoc` (129 distinct `markup.*`); Quarto `markup.bold.quarto`; MDX `markup.heading.atx.1.mdx` (and notably **no** `markup.bold.mdx` — emphasis lives under `string.other.strong.emphasis.*.mdx`, which is why MDX emphasis colours poorly); Org-mode `markup.heading.org`/`markup.bold.org`; LaTeX `markup.bold.textbf.latex`; reStructuredText uses **bare, unsuffixed** `markup.heading`/`markup.bold`/`markup.italic`/`markup.underline.link`.

**Vue, Twig, Jinja, Liquid name zero prose constructs.** They are pure delimiter grammars that compose by `include` or `injectionSelector`. Jinja's Markdown host grammar is 559 bytes and redefines nothing — it just includes `text.html.markdown` and derives `scopeName: text.html.markdown.jinja`.

**The one published golden principle** — asciidoctor-vscode PR #1018 (https://github.com/asciidoctor/asciidoctor-vscode/pull/1018), verbatim:

> "Use double scopes on list patterns (e.g. markup.list.unnumbered.asciidoc + markup.list.unnumbered.markdown) so default themes apply colors via the .markdown prefix while users can target the precise .asciidoc scope for customization."

That is the whole strategy in one sentence, and it is exactly the problem you are solving.

`meta.embedded.*` shape is **not** uniform: `meta.embedded.block.<lang>` (AsciiDoc, Quarto, Shopify Liquid, VS Code Markdown) vs flat `meta.embedded.<lang>` (MDX) vs absent (Vue, Twig, Jinja).

## (E) Multi-scope `name` fields — sanctioned by implementation, undocumented in prose

**No published prose statement exists.** The TextMate manual, the VS Code guide, and Aerijo's widely-cited grammar guide are all silent. Do not cite a doc for this; cite the engine.

`vscode-textmate`, `AttributedScopeStack.pushAttributed` (https://github.com/microsoft/vscode-textmate/blob/main/src/grammar/grammar.ts):

```ts
if (scopePath.indexOf(' ') === -1) {
    // This is the common case and much faster
    return AttributedScopeStack._pushAttributed(this, scopePath, grammar);
}
const scopes = scopePath.split(/ /g);
```

Space-separated scopes are split and pushed as **separate stack entries**, deliberately, with a fast path for the single case. Shiki inherits it.

First-party precedent: VS Code's own Markdown grammar ships `"contentName": "meta.embedded.block.cpp source.cpp"`. In the wild: AsciiDoc (5, including the two `markup.list.*.asciidoc markup.list.*.markdown` pairs), MDX (`markup.raw.code.mdx markup.inline.raw.code.mdx`), LaTeX (`meta.math.block.latex support.class.math.block.environment.latex`), and Quarto's generator, which treats appended scopes as a first-class facility.

**Unverified caveat:** search results claim Atom did not support space-separated multi-scopes. I could not check it (repo archived). Irrelevant for VS Code/Shiki targets, but I will not assert it either way.

**Your grammar already does this** — commit `169eaec` shipped `"markup.heading.1.tiddlywiki5 meta.heading.heading-1.tiddlywiki5"` and `"markup.inline.raw.double.tiddlywiki5 markup.raw.inline.tiddlywiki5"`. The practice is correct and well-precedented.

## (F) Recommendations, grounded in the grammar's current names

I measured `syntaxes/`. Ranked by measured colour-loss:

1. **`markup.other.*` is your second `meta.*` trap — 22 uses, 0/9 themes.** `markup.other.paragraph` (8×), `markup.other.table` (5×), `markup.other.style`, `markup.other.image`, `markup.other.variable.*`. Sanctioned by Sublime's doc, reached by nothing. Move each to a styled root or pair it: tables → `markup.table.tiddlywiki5` (VS Code Markdown's own name); images → `markup.underline.link.image.tiddlywiki5` (4/9); paragraph is genuinely structural → `meta.paragraph.*`, and accept it is unstyled, as Markdown does.
2. **`markup.underline.strikethrough.tiddlywiki5` is wrong and costs 6/9.** The effective name is `markup.strikethrough`; under `markup.underline` it inherits underline styling instead. Pair it: `markup.strikethrough.tiddlywiki5 markup.underline.strikethrough.tiddlywiki5`.
3. **`markup.link.tiddlywiki5` / `markup.link.external.tiddlywiki5` reach 1/9.** Canonical is `markup.underline.link` (4/9, plus 6/9 via the `markup.underline` parent). You already spell it correctly for syslinks and images — make the two odd ones out match.
4. **`markup.superscript` / `markup.subscript` reach 0/9** and no canonical name exists. Pair each with a styled carrier you can defend, or accept them as deliberately unstyled — but decide knowingly.
5. **Adopt the AsciiDoc dual-scope rule wherever the glyph matches Markdown.** Lists especially: `markup.list.unnumbered.ul.li.tiddlywiki5 markup.list.unnumbered.markdown`. `markup.list` alone reaches only 3/9, but the `.markdown` sibling picks up `dark_vs`'s `punctuation.definition.list.begin.markdown` rule — the same lever AsciiDoc pulled.
6. **Emit the three legacy aliases at the same positions** — bare `header`, `strong`, `emphasis` are still styled by `dark_vs`/`light_vs`, Dracula and One Dark Pro. Cheap, and it is why some older grammars still colour where newer ones do not.
7. **Add `markup.inserted` / `markup.deleted` / `markup.changed` (9/9)** if wikitext has any diff-shaped construct — that is the highest-reach triple you currently do not emit.
8. **Keep `meta.*` for structure only, and never as a construct's sole scope.** Your existing `meta.embedded.block.html.tiddlywiki5` and the paired heading names are exactly right; the pattern to generalise.
9. **Verify by measurement, not by doc.** The docs missed `markup.changed`, `markup.strikethrough`, `markup.fenced_code.block` and `markup.inline.raw`, and blessed `markup.other`, which nothing reaches. Your 65-theme harness is the authority here — the naming references are a starting hypothesis it should test.

One caution on `markup.fenced_code.block.tiddlywiki5`: it reaches only **1/9** on its own. It is the right structural name and every prior-art grammar uses it, but it is not what colours a code block — `markup.inline.raw` (9/9) and the embedded `source.*` do. Keep it, do not rely on it.

Sources: [Sublime scope naming](https://www.sublimetext.com/docs/scope_naming.html) · [TextMate language grammars](https://macromates.com/manual/en/language_grammars) · [VS Code syntax highlight guide](https://code.visualstudio.com/api/language-extensions/syntax-highlight-guide) · [vscode-textmate grammar.ts](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode-textmate/blob/main/src/grammar/grammar.ts) · [VS Code markdown grammar](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/microsoft/vscode/main/extensions/markdown-basics/syntaxes/markdown.tmLanguage.json) · [asciidoctor-vscode PR #1018](https://github.com/asciidoctor/asciidoctor-vscode/pull/1018) · [markdown-tm-language grammar.yml](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/wooorm/markdown-tm-language/main/grammar.yml) · [Quarto grammar](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarto-dev/quarto/main/apps/vscode/syntaxes/quarto.tmLanguage.yaml) · [Vue grammar](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/vuejs/language-tools/master/extensions/vscode/syntaxes/vue.tmLanguage.json) · [Shiki](https://shiki.style/guide/)

